 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551222 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551222 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4e59a97-7099-5d85-88ab-f4f7fe7124bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551224 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f732bcd0-c8c8-5638-93ed-8a183c2c0d4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3433ea61-fe1b-529a-a624-2ade0d70ec66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551259 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a9d38f9-b939-5805-88eb-ef96ffddb725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551262 #edb/ref 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3df63019-7de5-5b3e-afc6-d7a9e953df1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551266 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $L$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5238d337-5418-5670-995a-6cacdf9c34e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551278 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ad7a8de-9677-5fd6-a871-171f20886f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551286 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7abe0f71-31aa-5c86-a7a0-fa2df71ee9ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551294 #edb/ref 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7effc142-a727-5443-a7a6-2e2db67c4b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551296 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd52658d-d0dc-5c1e-b084-552fde414f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551345 #edb/ref 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
